WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.367
A boxplot provides a compact visual summary of a numeric distribution.

00:00:05.367 --> 00:00:08.892
The line inside the box represents the median.

00:00:08.892 --> 00:00:14.957
The box covers the middle fifty percent of the data, from Q1 to Q3.

00:00:14.957 --> 00:00:22.039
The whiskers extend beyond the box, and points beyond the whiskers may be flagged as potential outliers.

00:00:22.717 --> 00:00:27.994
Boxplots become especially useful when we place several groups side by side.

00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:32.414
Run the code and compare order amount across North, South, and West.

00:00:32.414 --> 00:00:37.119
Look at the medians and the spread within each region.

00:00:37.119 --> 00:00:41.379
Do not focus only on which group has the highest maximum.

00:00:41.379 --> 00:00:49.169
A useful comparison asks whether one group tends to be higher overall, whether one group has more variability,

00:00:49.169 --> 00:00:52.139
and whether any unusual values appear.

00:00:52.139 --> 00:00:56.952
The visual should support the same interpretation as your numerical summary.
